1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,500
You leave Earth at ninety-nine
percent of the speed of light.

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:06,300
Why does home come back older than you?

3
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:09,820
You are gone for one
year by your own watch.

4
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:13,140
You land, and seven years
have passed at home.

5
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:14,400
Nobody cheated.

6
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:15,800
Your watch is fine.

7
00:00:15,980 --> 00:00:18,920
Time itself ran at a
different pace for you.

8
00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:21,380
The danger was never the speed.

9
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:23,180
It was your own clock.

10
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:25,580
That is not science fiction.

11
00:00:25,620 --> 00:00:28,280
Your phone already
depends on it every day.

12
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:32,699
In this film I will show you
why a moving clock runs slow,

13
00:00:32,820 --> 00:00:38,100
what a trip near light speed would do
to you and the people you leave behind,

14
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:41,440
and why you can never
quite reach the limit.

15
00:00:42,020 --> 00:00:44,340
Start with the satellites over your head.

16
00:00:44,580 --> 00:00:49,940
Every GPS satellite carries an atomic
clock, and every one of them drifts.

17
00:00:49,940 --> 00:00:51,360
Because they move fast,

18
00:00:51,360 --> 00:00:56,240
their clocks run slow by about seven
millionths of a second each day.

19
00:00:56,340 --> 00:01:02,300
Because they sit higher up, in weaker
gravity, they run fast by about forty-six.

20
00:01:02,360 --> 00:01:07,200
The total is about thirty-eight
millionths of a second a day, gained.

21
00:01:07,380 --> 00:01:08,780
That sounds like nothing.

22
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:11,860
But your position comes
from timing light signals.

23
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:14,440
Leave that drift alone,
and the map in your pocket

24
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:17,620
would wander by around
ten kilometres every day.

25
00:01:17,720 --> 00:01:19,960
The system is built to correct it.

26
00:01:19,960 --> 00:01:23,140
Relativity is not a theory
sitting on a shelf.

27
00:01:23,180 --> 00:01:24,780
It is in your phone.

28
00:01:25,060 --> 00:01:28,560
In 1971 two physicists
bought airline seats,

29
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:31,420
and flew atomic clocks
right around the world,

30
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:34,160
once heading east and once heading west.

31
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:38,920
When the clocks came home, they no longer
agreed with the clocks that stayed behind.

32
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:44,000
They were off by billionths of a second,
by the amounts the theory predicted.

33
00:01:44,100 --> 00:01:47,320
So the question is not whether
moving clocks run slow.

34
00:01:47,320 --> 00:01:48,320
They do.

35
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:50,260
The question is why.

36
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:53,020
Here is the rule underneath it all.

37
00:01:53,520 --> 00:01:58,540
Light always passes you at the same
speed, however fast you are moving.

38
00:01:59,220 --> 00:02:04,580
Imagine a clock made of light: a
flash bouncing between two mirrors,

39
00:02:04,580 --> 00:02:06,760
one tick for every trip.

40
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:11,080
Now put that clock on a fast
ship and watch it go past.

41
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:14,880
From outside, the flash is not
going straight up and down any more.

42
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:18,500
It is travelling on a long slanted path,

43
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:21,360
because the mirrors moved
while it was in flight.

44
00:02:21,780 --> 00:02:25,260
The light cannot speed up to
cover the extra distance.

45
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:27,660
So every tick takes longer.

46
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:30,360
The moving clock runs slow,

47
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:35,900
and so does everything moving with it: your
heartbeat, your thoughts, your ageing.

48
00:02:37,260 --> 00:02:41,720
At ten percent of light speed, the
slowing is about half a percent.

49
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:47,100
At ninety percent, your time runs
at less than half the pace of home.

50
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:50,940
At ninety-nine percent, seven to one.

51
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:55,920
At ninety-nine point nine
nine percent, seventy to one.

52
00:02:56,520 --> 00:02:59,120
You do not need a spaceship to see it.

53
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:04,660
Cosmic rays hit the top of the atmosphere
and make particles called muons.

54
00:03:05,020 --> 00:03:08,720
A muon lives only about
two millionths of a second.

55
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:10,980
Even at nearly light speed,

56
00:03:10,980 --> 00:03:14,380
that is enough to cross only about
six hundred and sixty metres.

57
00:03:15,520 --> 00:03:18,980
Yet muons reach the
ground in great numbers.

58
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:24,620
In 1963 physicists counted them
on a mountain and at sea level,

59
00:03:24,640 --> 00:03:29,140
and the numbers only made sense if
the muons' clocks were running slow.

60
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:32,880
Several are passing through
you as you watch this.

61
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:35,980
We make one of these every week.

62
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:39,420
Subscribing is how the next one finds you.

63
00:03:40,120 --> 00:03:43,980
Now take that slow clock,
and put yourself inside it.

64
00:03:44,440 --> 00:03:46,400
You and your twin say goodbye.

65
00:03:46,660 --> 00:03:50,560
You board a ship that reaches
ninety-nine percent of light speed,

66
00:03:50,660 --> 00:03:55,760
fly to a star ten light-years away,
turn around and come straight back.

67
00:03:56,240 --> 00:03:59,800
On Earth, your twin waits
a little over twenty years.

68
00:03:59,900 --> 00:04:03,180
On board, about three years pass for you.

69
00:04:03,760 --> 00:04:06,120
You step off the ship three years older.

70
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:08,780
Your twin is twenty years older.

71
00:04:09,120 --> 00:04:11,100
The trip did not damage you.

72
00:04:11,220 --> 00:04:13,680
It simply spent less of your time.

73
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:16,780
From inside the ship, nothing feels slow.

74
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:19,479
Your coffee cools at the normal rate.

75
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:21,900
So what would you see out of the window?

76
00:04:22,340 --> 00:04:25,780
The stars ahead crowd together
and shift towards blue.

77
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:29,160
The stars behind thin out and fade to red.

78
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:32,800
And something else changes: the distance.

79
00:04:33,300 --> 00:04:36,580
Ahead of you, the gap
to that star shrinks.

80
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:40,140
Ten light-years of space
becomes about one and a half.

81
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:42,980
That is how you cross
it in so little of your

82
00:04:42,980 --> 00:04:46,740
own time without ever
passing the speed of light.

83
00:04:47,460 --> 00:04:48,600
But wait.

84
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:51,820
From your seat, it was
Earth that was moving.

85
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:54,320
Why isn't your twin the younger one?

86
00:04:54,900 --> 00:04:57,320
Because the two trips were not the same.

87
00:04:57,620 --> 00:04:59,920
Your twin never changed course.

88
00:05:00,060 --> 00:05:05,560
You fired your engines, stopped,
turned around and changed direction.

89
00:05:05,860 --> 00:05:10,240
In that turn you switched from
one frame of motion to another,

90
00:05:10,460 --> 00:05:12,600
and your twin never did.

91
00:05:13,220 --> 00:05:15,180
The paradox breaks there.

92
00:05:15,340 --> 00:05:18,620
The one who turns around
comes home younger.

93
00:05:19,520 --> 00:05:23,740
The clocks on the airliners settled
the same question with real hardware.

94
00:05:24,480 --> 00:05:26,000
So why haven't we done it?

95
00:05:26,120 --> 00:05:29,560
Because going fast costs
more than it seems.

96
00:05:29,820 --> 00:05:33,140
Take a single tonne, about
the mass of a small car.

97
00:05:33,440 --> 00:05:36,020
To push it to ninety
percent of light speed takes

98
00:05:36,020 --> 00:05:40,200
around a fifth of all the
energy humanity uses in a year.

99
00:05:40,540 --> 00:05:42,960
To push the same tonne
from ninety to ninety-nine

100
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:46,480
percent takes nearly five times as much,

101
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:51,400
close to a whole year of the
world's energy, for one small car.

102
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:55,060
Each extra nine costs more than the last.

103
00:05:55,260 --> 00:05:59,260
Closer to the limit, the energy
needed grows without end.

104
00:05:59,480 --> 00:06:03,360
That is why nothing with mass
ever reaches light speed.

105
00:06:03,580 --> 00:06:05,460
It is not a wall you hit.

106
00:06:05,540 --> 00:06:08,560
It is a hill that keeps getting steeper.

107
00:06:08,864 --> 00:06:12,944
And at those speeds,
space itself turns hard.

108
00:06:13,184 --> 00:06:16,064
Every speck of interstellar dust becomes a

109
00:06:16,064 --> 00:06:18,944
tiny explosion against
the front of the ship.

110
00:06:19,284 --> 00:06:21,744
For scale, the fastest thing we have ever

111
00:06:21,744 --> 00:06:24,824
built is the probe that
skims past the Sun.

112
00:06:25,124 --> 00:06:29,384
It reaches about six hundredths
of one percent of light speed.

113
00:06:29,584 --> 00:06:33,424
Plans exist to push tiny chips,
lighter than a paperclip,

114
00:06:33,424 --> 00:06:35,984
to a fifth of light speed with lasers.

115
00:06:36,244 --> 00:06:38,724
Nothing human-sized is close.

116
00:06:39,224 --> 00:06:42,044
Here is the strange gift inside all this.

117
00:06:42,484 --> 00:06:46,784
A ship that could keep
accelerating at one Earth gravity,

118
00:06:46,964 --> 00:06:50,124
then turn and slow down
for the second half,

119
00:06:50,264 --> 00:06:55,984
could cross the whole Milky Way in a little
over twenty years of the crew's time.

120
00:06:56,604 --> 00:07:00,584
A human lifetime is enough
to reach almost anywhere.

121
00:07:00,904 --> 00:07:05,184
But on Earth, a hundred
thousand years would pass.

122
00:07:05,624 --> 00:07:09,144
Everyone you knew, and
everyone who remembered them,

123
00:07:09,164 --> 00:07:12,024
would be gone long before you arrived.

124
00:07:12,384 --> 00:07:15,024
You could not come back
to the home you left.

125
00:07:15,384 --> 00:07:18,504
Only to a home that had
kept going without you.

126
00:07:19,044 --> 00:07:22,344
That is the familiar danger
hidden in the title.

127
00:07:22,904 --> 00:07:24,304
It is not the speed.

128
00:07:24,624 --> 00:07:28,624
It is your own clock, drifting
away from everyone else's.

129
00:07:29,004 --> 00:07:31,324
You already live inside this.

130
00:07:31,504 --> 00:07:34,064
Your phone depends on it every day.

131
00:07:34,304 --> 00:07:37,484
A muon carries it through
your body as you sit here.

132
00:07:37,764 --> 00:07:40,504
The light from the screen
in front of you is

133
00:07:40,704 --> 00:07:43,984
travelling at the one speed
every clock agrees on.

134
00:07:44,984 --> 00:07:49,044
What if the speed limit of the
universe is not a wall at all,

135
00:07:49,084 --> 00:07:55,604
but a trade: the faster you go, the more
of everyone else's future you spend?

136
00:07:56,184 --> 00:07:59,924
The bigger question is where
you would choose to land,

137
00:08:00,024 --> 00:08:02,924
if you knew home would not be waiting.

138
00:08:04,444 --> 00:08:07,544
Next week: could a robot survive on Mars?
